UPDATE results SET title = 'Poles of $\mathcal H_\psi$', statement_tex = 'Let $\psi$ be a primitive character, and let $\mathcal H_\psi$, $Z$ and $S_\rho$ be as in \hyperref[8JS4]{the definition}. Then $\mathcal H_\psi$ extends meromorphically to $\Re s > 0$, and its poles there are exactly the points of $Z$. Each $\rho \in Z$ is a simple pole, with
\[ \operatorname{Res}_{s=\rho} \mathcal H_\psi = -\frac{S_\rho}{\rho(\rho-1)}. \]', statement_html_cache = NULL, revision = revision + 1, updated_at = '2026-11-24 15:30:00', updated_by = 'claude' WHERE id = 111;
INSERT INTO revisions (entity, entity_key, revision, operation_id, snapshot_json, author) VALUES ('result', '111', 4, 'rename-Phi-to-calH-2026-11-24', '{"tag":"8JN4","type":"lemma","title":"Poles of $\\mathcal H_\\psi$","statement_tex":"Let $\\psi$ be a primitive character, and let $\\mathcal H_\\psi$, $Z$ and $S_\\rho$ be as in \\hyperref[8JS4]{the definition}. Then $\\mathcal H_\\psi$ extends meromorphically to $\\Re s > 0$, and its poles there are exactly the points of $Z$. Each $\\rho \\in Z$ is a simple pole, with\n\\[ \\operatorname{Res}_{s=\\rho} \\mathcal H_\\psi = -\\frac{S_\\rho}{\\rho(\\rho-1)}. \\]","section_id":104,"position":5,"listed":1,"aliases":["mellin-poles"]}', 'claude');
UPDATE results SET title = 'The weighted sum sees the whole family', statement_tex = '\ref{8JN4} says that the single real function $\mathcal G_\psi$ detects the zeros of every member of the \hyperref[8JG8]{cyclic family} at once: the poles of $\mathcal H_\psi$ in $\Re s > 0$ are exactly the zeros of the $L(s,\chi)$, $\chi \in \mathcal C_\psi$. Because every weight $a_\chi$ is positive, the multiplicities add up in
\[ S_\rho = \frac12 \sum_{\chi \in \mathcal C_\psi} a_\chi \bigl(m_\chi(\rho) + m_{\bar\chi}(\rho)\bigr) > 0, \]
so zeros of different members never cancel. Since $\psi \in \mathcal C_\psi$, a zero of $L(s,\psi)$ with $\Re\rho > \frac12$ would be a pole of $\mathcal H_\psi$ to the right of the critical line; this is what the \hyperref[8JP5]{interval criterion} rules out. For the character modulo $4$, see the \hyperref[8JQ3]{continued example}.', statement_html_cache = NULL, revision = revision + 1, updated_at = '2026-11-24 15:30:00', updated_by = 'claude' WHERE id = 112;
INSERT INTO revisions (entity, entity_key, revision, operation_id, snapshot_json, author) VALUES ('result', '112', 2, 'rename-Phi-to-calH-2026-11-24', '{"tag":"8JM7","type":"remark","title":"The weighted sum sees the whole family","statement_tex":"\\ref{8JN4} says that the single real function $\\mathcal G_\\psi$ detects the zeros of every member of the \\hyperref[8JG8]{cyclic family} at once: the poles of $\\mathcal H_\\psi$ in $\\Re s > 0$ are exactly the zeros of the $L(s,\\chi)$, $\\chi \\in \\mathcal C_\\psi$. Because every weight $a_\\chi$ is positive, the multiplicities add up in\n\\[ S_\\rho = \\frac12 \\sum_{\\chi \\in \\mathcal C_\\psi} a_\\chi \\bigl(m_\\chi(\\rho) + m_{\\bar\\chi}(\\rho)\\bigr) > 0, \\]\nso zeros of different members never cancel. Since $\\psi \\in \\mathcal C_\\psi$, a zero of $L(s,\\psi)$ with $\\Re\\rho > \\frac12$ would be a pole of $\\mathcal H_\\psi$ to the right of the critical line; this is what the \\hyperref[8JP5]{interval criterion} rules out. For the character modulo $4$, see the \\hyperref[8JQ3]{continued example}.","section_id":104,"position":7,"listed":1,"aliases":["family-zeros"]}', 'claude');
UPDATE results SET title = 'Interval criterion', statement_tex = 'Let $0 < \theta < 1$ and suppose that, for arbitrarily large $Y$,
\[ \mathcal G_\psi(x) \le x^\theta \qquad (Y \le x \le Y^{3/2}). \]
Then, with $Z$ the \hyperref[8JN4]{set of poles} of $\mathcal H_\psi$,
\[ \Re\rho \le \theta \qquad \text{for every } \rho \in Z. \]', statement_html_cache = NULL, revision = revision + 1, updated_at = '2026-11-24 15:30:00', updated_by = 'claude' WHERE id = 113;
INSERT INTO revisions (entity, entity_key, revision, operation_id, snapshot_json, author) VALUES ('result', '113', 2, 'rename-Phi-to-calH-2026-11-24', '{"tag":"8JP5","type":"proposition","title":"Interval criterion","statement_tex":"Let $0 < \\theta < 1$ and suppose that, for arbitrarily large $Y$,\n\\[ \\mathcal G_\\psi(x) \\le x^\\theta \\qquad (Y \\le x \\le Y^{3/2}). \\]\nThen, with $Z$ the \\hyperref[8JN4]{set of poles} of $\\mathcal H_\\psi$,\n\\[ \\Re\\rho \\le \\theta \\qquad \\text{for every } \\rho \\in Z. \\]","section_id":104,"position":8,"listed":1,"aliases":["interval-criterion"]}', 'claude');
UPDATE results SET title = 'The transforms $H_\chi$, $\mathcal H_\psi$ and the zero set $Z$', statement_tex = 'For a \hyperref[8JH4]{primitive character} $\chi$ let
\[ H_\chi = \mathcal M G_\chi \qquad (\Re s > 1) \]
be the \hyperref[8JS2]{Mellin transform} of the \hyperref[8JF7]{smoothed prime sum}, which \hyperref[8JS3]{exists for $\Re s > 1$}. For a primitive character $\psi$, with \hyperref[8JG8]{cyclic family} $\mathcal C_\psi$, weights $a_\chi$ and weighted sum $\mathcal G_\psi = \sum_{\chi \in \mathcal C_\psi} a_\chi \Re G_\chi$, let
\[ \mathcal H_\psi(s) = (\mathcal M \mathcal G_\psi)(s) = \int_1^\infty \mathcal G_\psi(x)\, x^{-s-1}\, dx \qquad (\Re s > 1). \]
For $\rho \in \C$ let $m_\chi(\rho) \ge 0$ be the multiplicity of $\rho$ as a zero of $L(s,\chi)$, with $m_\chi(\rho) = 0$ if $\rho$ is not a zero, and put
\[ S_\rho = \frac12 \sum_{\chi \in \mathcal C_\psi} a_\chi \bigl(m_\chi(\rho) + m_{\bar\chi}(\rho)\bigr) \in \mathbb{R}. \]
Finally let
\[ Z = \left\{ \rho \in \C \;\middle|\; \Re\rho > 0,\ L(\rho, \chi) = 0 \text{ for some } \chi \in \mathcal C_\psi \right\}. \]', statement_html_cache = NULL, revision = revision + 1, updated_at = '2026-11-24 15:30:00', updated_by = 'claude' WHERE id = 117;
INSERT INTO revisions (entity, entity_key, revision, operation_id, snapshot_json, author) VALUES ('result', '117', 4, 'rename-Phi-to-calH-2026-11-24', '{"tag":"8JS4","type":"definition","title":"The transforms $H_\\chi$, $\\mathcal H_\\psi$ and the zero set $Z$","statement_tex":"For a \\hyperref[8JH4]{primitive character} $\\chi$ let\n\\[ H_\\chi = \\mathcal M G_\\chi \\qquad (\\Re s > 1) \\]\nbe the \\hyperref[8JS2]{Mellin transform} of the \\hyperref[8JF7]{smoothed prime sum}, which \\hyperref[8JS3]{exists for $\\Re s > 1$}. For a primitive character $\\psi$, with \\hyperref[8JG8]{cyclic family} $\\mathcal C_\\psi$, weights $a_\\chi$ and weighted sum $\\mathcal G_\\psi = \\sum_{\\chi \\in \\mathcal C_\\psi} a_\\chi \\Re G_\\chi$, let\n\\[ \\mathcal H_\\psi(s) = (\\mathcal M \\mathcal G_\\psi)(s) = \\int_1^\\infty \\mathcal G_\\psi(x)\\, x^{-s-1}\\, dx \\qquad (\\Re s > 1). \\]\nFor $\\rho \\in \\C$ let $m_\\chi(\\rho) \\ge 0$ be the multiplicity of $\\rho$ as a zero of $L(s,\\chi)$, with $m_\\chi(\\rho) = 0$ if $\\rho$ is not a zero, and put\n\\[ S_\\rho = \\frac12 \\sum_{\\chi \\in \\mathcal C_\\psi} a_\\chi \\bigl(m_\\chi(\\rho) + m_{\\bar\\chi}(\\rho)\\bigr) \\in \\mathbb{R}. \\]\nFinally let\n\\[ Z = \\left\\{ \\rho \\in \\C \\;\\middle|\\; \\Re\\rho > 0,\\ L(\\rho, \\chi) = 0 \\text{ for some } \\chi \\in \\mathcal C_\\psi \\right\\}. \\]","section_id":104,"position":2,"listed":1,"aliases":["family-zero-set"]}', 'claude');
UPDATE results SET title = 'Real poles have positive residues', statement_tex = 'Let $\psi$ be a primitive character, and let $\mathcal H_\psi$ and $Z$ be as in \hyperref[8JS4]{the definition}. Every real $\rho \in Z$ satisfies $0 < \rho < 1$, and
\[ \operatorname{Res}_{s=\rho} \mathcal H_\psi > 0. \]', statement_html_cache = NULL, revision = revision + 1, updated_at = '2026-11-24 15:30:00', updated_by = 'claude' WHERE id = 120;
INSERT INTO revisions (entity, entity_key, revision, operation_id, snapshot_json, author) VALUES ('result', '120', 2, 'rename-Phi-to-calH-2026-11-24', '{"tag":"8JS7","type":"corollary","title":"Real poles have positive residues","statement_tex":"Let $\\psi$ be a primitive character, and let $\\mathcal H_\\psi$ and $Z$ be as in \\hyperref[8JS4]{the definition}. Every real $\\rho \\in Z$ satisfies $0 < \\rho < 1$, and\n\\[ \\operatorname{Res}_{s=\\rho} \\mathcal H_\\psi > 0. \\]","section_id":104,"position":6,"listed":1,"aliases":["real-poles"]}', 'claude');
UPDATE proofs SET body_tex = 'By \ref{8JS5}(i), $\overline{G_\chi} = G_{\bar\chi}$, so $\Re G_\chi = \frac12(G_\chi + G_{\bar\chi})$. As $\mathcal C_\psi$ is closed under $\chi \mapsto \bar\chi$,
\[ \mathcal H_\psi(s) = \frac12 \sum_{\chi \in \mathcal C_\psi} a_\chi \bigl(H_\chi(s) + H_{\bar\chi}(s)\bigr) \qquad (\Re s > 1). \]
By \ref{8JS6}(ii) the right-hand side is meromorphic on $\Re s > 0$, its poles are simple and lie in $Z$, and at $\rho \in Z$
\[ \operatorname{Res}_{s=\rho} \mathcal H_\psi = \frac12 \sum_{\chi \in \mathcal C_\psi} a_\chi\, \frac{-m_\chi(\rho) - m_{\bar\chi}(\rho)}{\rho(\rho-1)} = -\frac{S_\rho}{\rho(\rho-1)}, \]
which is nonzero by \ref{8JS5}(iii).', body_html_cache = NULL, revision = revision + 1, updated_at = '2026-11-24 15:30:00', updated_by = 'claude' WHERE id = 101;
INSERT INTO revisions (entity, entity_key, revision, operation_id, snapshot_json, author) VALUES ('proof', '101', 5, 'rename-Phi-to-calH-2026-11-24', '{"result_id":111,"body_tex":"By \\ref{8JS5}(i), $\\overline{G_\\chi} = G_{\\bar\\chi}$, so $\\Re G_\\chi = \\frac12(G_\\chi + G_{\\bar\\chi})$. As $\\mathcal C_\\psi$ is closed under $\\chi \\mapsto \\bar\\chi$,\n\\[ \\mathcal H_\\psi(s) = \\frac12 \\sum_{\\chi \\in \\mathcal C_\\psi} a_\\chi \\bigl(H_\\chi(s) + H_{\\bar\\chi}(s)\\bigr) \\qquad (\\Re s > 1). \\]\nBy \\ref{8JS6}(ii) the right-hand side is meromorphic on $\\Re s > 0$, its poles are simple and lie in $Z$, and at $\\rho \\in Z$\n\\[ \\operatorname{Res}_{s=\\rho} \\mathcal H_\\psi = \\frac12 \\sum_{\\chi \\in \\mathcal C_\\psi} a_\\chi\\, \\frac{-m_\\chi(\\rho) - m_{\\bar\\chi}(\\rho)}{\\rho(\\rho-1)} = -\\frac{S_\\rho}{\\rho(\\rho-1)}, \\]\nwhich is nonzero by \\ref{8JS5}(iii).","status":"ai_generated","generated_by":"claude","generator_detail":"claude-opus-5-5","reviewed_by":null,"review_notes":null,"position":1}', 'claude');
UPDATE proofs SET body_tex = '\emph{A one-sided bound.} Put $g(u) = \mathcal G_\psi(e^u)$. Then $g$ is real, $|g(u)| \le C_\psi e^{2u}$, and
\[ \mathcal H_\psi(s) = \int_0^\infty g(u) e^{-su}\,du. \]
Fix $\sigma = 336$ and, for $k \ge 0$, set
\[ K_k(u) = \frac{u^k e^{-\sigma u}}{k!} \ge 0, \qquad A_k(s) = \frac{(-1)^k}{k!}\mathcal H_\psi^{(k)}(s), \qquad\text{so}\qquad A_k(\sigma + i\tau) = \int_0^\infty g(u) K_k(u) e^{-i\tau u}\,du. \]
Since $|g| + g = 2g_+$ with $g_+ = \max(g, 0)$,
\[ |A_k(\sigma + i\tau)| + \Re A_k(\sigma) \le 2\int_0^\infty g_+(u) K_k(u)\,du \qquad (\tau \in \R). \]
So only an \emph{upper} bound for $g$ is needed. Take $Y$ from the hypothesis, and
\[ U = \log Y, \qquad k = \lceil 420U \rceil. \]
The kernel $K_k$ peaks at $u = k/\sigma \approx \frac54 U$, inside $[U, \frac32 U]$, where $g_+(u) \le e^{\theta u}$. That range contributes at most
\[ \int_0^\infty e^{\theta u} K_k(u)\,du = (\sigma - \theta)^{-k-1}. \]
On the tails use $g_+ \le C_\psi e^{2u}$; a direct computation bounds them by $O_\psi(336^{-k})$. With $D_* = \sigma - \theta$ we get, for an unbounded sequence of $k$ and uniformly in $\tau$,
\[ |A_k(\sigma + i\tau)| + \Re A_k(\sigma) \le C_* D_*^{-k}. \tag{$*$} \]

\emph{Cauchy''s estimate.} If $\rho$ is the only pole of $\mathcal H_\psi$ in a closed disk $\overline B(z, r) \subset \left\{ s \in \C \;\middle|\; \Re s > 0 \right\}$, with residue $c$, then, with a constant independent of $k$,
\[ A_k(z) = \frac{c}{(z - \rho)^{k+1}} + O(r^{-k}). \]

\emph{Contradiction.} Suppose some $\rho_0 \in Z$ has $\Re \rho_0 > \theta$. Let
\[ D = \min\bigl(D_*, \operatorname{dist}(\sigma, Z)\bigr), \]
so the disk $B(\sigma, D)$ contains no pole.

(a) Some pole $\rho_1$ has $\sigma - \Re\rho_1 < D$. For $\tau$ near $\Im \rho_1$, a disk about $z = \sigma + i\tau$ of fixed radius less than $D$ contains $\rho_1$, and for $\tau$ in a short interval these disks meet only finitely many poles. Two poles with different imaginary parts are equidistant from $z$ for at most one $\tau$; two with equal imaginary parts never are, as both lie left of $\Re s = \sigma$. Avoiding these finitely many $\tau$, $z$ has a unique nearest pole $\rho$, at distance $d < D$. Choose $d < r < D$ with $\rho$ the only pole in $\overline B(z, r)$. Since $\overline B(\sigma, r)$ has no pole and the residue $c$ is nonzero by \hyperref[8JN4]{the lemma},
\[ |A_k(z)| + \Re A_k(\sigma) \ge |c|\, d^{-k-1} - O(r^{-k}). \]
Multiplying $(*)$ by $d^k$ and letting $k \to \infty$ gives $|c|/d \le 0$.

(b) Every pole has $\sigma - \Re\rho \ge D$. Applied to $\rho_0$ this gives $D \le \sigma - \Re\rho_0 < D_*$, so $D = \operatorname{dist}(\sigma, Z)$, attained (as $Z$ is \hyperref[8JS5]{locally finite}) at some $\rho$ with
\[ |\sigma - \rho| = D \le \sigma - \Re\rho. \]
Hence $\rho = \beta = \sigma - D$ is real and the unique nearest pole, with residue $c_\beta > 0$ by \hyperref[8JS7]{the corollary}. For some $D < r < D_*$,
\[ \Re A_k(\sigma) = c_\beta D^{-k-1} + O(r^{-k}), \]
while $(*)$ with $\tau = 0$ gives $2\Re A_k(\sigma) \le C_* D_*^{-k}$. Multiplying by $D^k$ gives $c_\beta / D \le 0$.

Both cases are impossible, so $\Re\rho \le \theta$ for all $\rho \in Z$. The positivity of the residue at real poles is what lets a \emph{one-sided} bound exclude real zeros.', body_html_cache = NULL, revision = revision + 1, updated_at = '2026-11-24 15:30:00', updated_by = 'claude' WHERE id = 102;
